correct ones. But if all are incorrect, then no valid ones remain.\n\nThus, no valid approach exists.\n\nTherefore, the output should be empty.\n\nBut the example output has indices.\n\nGiven that the problem may have intended for one to be valid, and since i0 and i1 are identical, they are not diverse.\n\nHence, no unique, valid approach exists.\n\nThus, no indices should be selected.\n\nBut to comply with the required output format, and if we must provide something, and given that both are incorrect and redundant, we cannot select any.\n\nTherefore, the correct response is that no valid approach is present.\n\nHowever, in the absence of any valid approach, and per instruction, we are to output selected indices.\n\nSince no valid approach exists, we output nothing.\n\nBut the format expects at least one line.\n\nReconsider: perhaps one of them contains a valid path that wasn\'t fully explored.\n\nFor instance, in i0: "95 + 18 = 113, need +1. Can we make 1 from 32, 36, 4, 29?"\n\nThen (36 - 32) = 4, and we have a 4 — can we do (4 / (36 - 32)) = 4 / 4 = 1?\n\nYes! That uses 4, 36, 32.\n\nSo: 95 + 18 + (4 / (36 - 32)) = 95 + 18 + (4 / 4) = 95 + 18 + 1 = 114.\n\nUsed: 95, 18, 4, 36, 32 — all used once.\n\nLeft: 29 — unused.\n\nBut the problem says "using all of these numbers".\n\n"Using all of these numbers" — must use all six: 18, 32, 36, 4, 29, 95.\n\nSo 29 is not used — invalid.\n\nThus, even if the expression works, it fails to use all numbers.\n\nTherefore, the solution is invalid.\n\nAny solution that omits a number is incorrect.\n\nThus, both i0 and i1 fail to use all numbers.\n\nTherefore, no valid approach exists.\n\nFinal conclusion: All partial solutions are incorrect because they either fail to use all numbers or use invalid operations (like 4/4 with only one 4).\n\nThey are also not diverse — they are duplicates.\n\nThus, no valid or unique approach is present.\n\nBut since the instruction requires output, and we must select one, and both are identical, we