he *only* one that correctly identifies a working core structure (56 * 9 - (71 - 59) = 492) is i3 — even though it acknowledges the missing numbers. But it does not provide a full valid expression.\n\nWait — the requirement is to use *all* numbers exactly once. Therefore, any solution that omits two numbers is invalid.\n\nBut perhaps there is a valid way to use the extra numbers in a neutral operation (e.g., + (74 - 74)) — which would not change the value.\n\nSo, if i3 had proposed:  \n56 * 9 - (71 - 59) + (74 - 74)  \nor  \n56 * 9 - (71 - 59) + (44 - 44)\n\nThen it would be valid and use all numbers.\n\nHowever, in the provided text, i3 only says: "We need to use 74 and 44" and stops. It does not actually write the full expression.\n\nTherefore, none of the partial solutions provide a complete, valid expression that uses all numbers.\n\nBut the instruction is to *identify the unique, valid approaches* from the partial solutions — implying that at least one must be correct.\n\nRe-examining: the only one that *correctly identifies* the core arithmetic path (56 * 9 - (71 - 59) = 492) and *recognizes* the need to incorporate the remaining numbers is i3. The others either fail to identify the 12 or stop short.\n\nThus, despite being incomplete, i3 contains the only correct and logical path that can be extended to a valid solution by adding a zero term (e.g., + (74 - 74)).\n\nSimilarly, i4, i6, i7 all follow the same path — they are duplicates of i3\'s idea but without the full realization.\n\nTherefore, i3 is the only one that demonstrates a correct and unique approach, even if not fully completed.\n\nBut the others are either redundant or flawed.\n\nNow, are there any others with a different approach?\n\ni5 attempts 71 * 7 - 5 = 492. That’s a different path. If it can build 7 and 5 from the other numbers, it could work.\n\nCan we build 7 and 5?\n\nWe have: 74, 44, 56, 9, 59.\n\nCan we make 7? 56 / 8 = 7 — can we make 8? 44 - 36? 9 - 2? 59 - 51? No clear path.\n\nCan we