l way? For example, multiply by (17/17) = 1?\n\nYes! That would be valid.\n\nSo: 36 × (59 - 21 - 12) × (17 / 17) = 936\n\nThis uses all numbers: 36, 59, 21, 12, 17, 17.\n\nThis expression is valid.\n\nNow, which of the partial solutions includes this idea?\n\ni0: proposes (59 - 21 - 12) = 26 → then 36 × 26 → but does not include the second 17. It stops short.\n\ni1: proposes (21 + 17 - 12) = 26 → same issue.\n\ni2: tries multiple combinations but does not mention (17/17) or any neutral term.\n\ni3: cuts off mid-sentence.\n\nTherefore, none of the partial solutions include the idea of using (17/17) to neutralize the extra 17s.\n\nSo all are incorrect in that they fail to use all numbers.\n\nBut the only way to use all numbers is to include a neutral operation like (17/17) = 1.\n\nThus, no partial solution presents a valid, complete expression.\n\nTherefore, there are no valid approaches among the given partial solutions.\n\nBut the instruction says to "identify the unique, valid approaches" — implying at least one exists.\n\nGiven that, and that i0 and i1 are the most developed, and i2 is the most diverse, but still invalid, and none use all numbers, we must conclude that no valid approach is present.\n\nHowever, since the task requires selecting one, and i2 is the only one that tries multiple paths (36×21, 36×17, etc.), it is the most diverse in method, even if flawed.\n\nThus, despite being incorrect, it is the most diverse.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem allows using numbers in a way that doesn\'t require all to be in a primary operation — but the requirement is "using all of these numbers".\n\nTherefore, any solution missing a number is invalid.\n\nThus, all are invalid.\n\nBut since we must output one, and the instruction says "select 1 of the most diverse approaches", we select the one that shows the most variety in thinking.\n\ni2 explores multiple multiplicative combinations, whereas i0 and i1 are focused on a single path.\n\nTherefore, i2 is the most 